From ba5a2c5e26d442b1ed5e0c3b4b72190324e209a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JohnnyC96 <54557697+JohnnyC96@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 09:12:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] IOP: cheaper per-instruction checks in the interpreter loop

The interpreter did three pieces of bookkeeping after every IOP
instruction, almost always for nothing:
- schedulePendingDma(): an out-of-line call that copied and cleared the
  memory's optional DMA start, although only a store to an SPU2 DMA
  CHCR can set it. Now IopMemory::hasDmaStart() (inline) is tested at
  the call site in step() and inside schedulePendingDma().
- servicePendingDmaInterrupts(): walked the pending-DMA map after every
  instruction while any DMA was pending, which with SPU2 streams taking
  playback time (patch 0015) is most of the time. Now the earliest
  completion cycle is kept (nextDmaCompletionCycle, recomputed when an
  entry is added, replaced or completed) and the service runs only when
  totalCycles reaches it. Guest callbacks likewise only when the first
  is due.
- imports.decode(pc): an out-of-line call that reads the word at pc and
  returns nothing unless it is jr ra. Now that word is tested inline for
  an aligned RAM pc (IopMemory::ramWord) before decode is called.
- IopMemory::physicalAddress, called twice per instruction by step(), is
  now inline in the header.

The service calls do nothing when nothing is due, so the IOP executes
the same instructions with the same cycle counts.
IopSubsystem/IopEmulator::setFastLoop(false) restores the full checks;
the runtime sets it for PS2X_IOP_FASTLOOP=0 (stderr "[iop] fast loop
off"), for A/B speed runs.

Tests (ps2_iop_batch_tests): an IRX thread starts a block SPU2 DMA
(16 bytes, 3072 cycles) and a voice DMA (64 cycles) with CPU stores and
counts its MADR polls until each completes: 17 and 752 polls,
8017 instructions, the same with the fast loop on and off, batched and
per checkpoint; a DelayThread wake (import stubs) is seen at the same
EE cycle and a spinning thread runs the same instructions both ways.
A speed-only change: these tests pass without it too (the project's
fail-without rule is waived for this patch by the developer); breaking
the deadline update makes the DMA test fail.
